   "t": "Да, я это уже знаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, I already know that.' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'know' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Eleonora answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Денис, а вы чем занимаетесь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Denis, and what do you do for a living?' In Russian, the idea of 'Denis' flows naturally into 'you do' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Я здесь у вас работаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I work right here for you.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'work' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Я работаю поваром на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I work as a cook in the kitchen.' Denis anchors the line on 'I', while 'kitchen' completes the thought. Eleonora answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Очень хорошо, и к Кате поближе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Very good, and closer to Katya.' The grammar packs 'very' and 'closer' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Вы теперь под моей крышей.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are under my roof now.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'roof' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Значит, будете часто видеться.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
